## `modules/app-infra/secrets.tf`

The connection string never exists in Git. It is composed at apply time from a generated password and stored in Secrets Manager; only the ARN is exported.

```hcl
resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret" "db" {
  name                    = "${local.name}/db/connection"
  description             = "Postgres connection string for ${local.name}"
  kms_key_id              = local.kms_key_arn
  recovery_window_in_days = local.is_prod ? 30 : 0
  tags                    = local.tags
}

resource "aws_secretsmanager_secret_version" "db" {
  secret_id = aws_secretsmanager_secret.db.id
  secret_string = jsonencode({
    engine   = "postgres"
    host     = aws_db_instance.this.address
    port     = aws_db_instance.this.port
    dbname   = aws_db_instance.this.db_name
    username = var.db_username
    password = random_password.db.result
    url      = format(
      "postgresql://%s:%s@%s:%d/%s?sslmode=require",
      var.db_username,
      urlencode(random_password.db.result),
      aws_db_instance.this.address,
      aws_db_instance.this.port,
      aws_db_instance.this.db_name,
    )
  })
}
```

> **State handling.** `random_password` and the secret value land in Terraform state. The backend below is therefore an S3 bucket with SSE-KMS + versioning and a locking table; state access is the same trust boundary as the database itself.

